# Disconnect-Safe Execution

The execution phase of any setup/reconfiguration script in this repo must finish on its own if the operator's terminal goes away (SSH drop, Raspberry Pi Connect WebRTC failure, serial hang-up, laptop closed). A half-applied state on a remote Pi can be unrecoverable without on-site help.

Rules:

- After the input phase ends, the script MUST detach from its controlling terminal:
  - `trap '' HUP` so SIGHUP is ignored (inherited as ignored by all child processes).
  - Close stdin (`exec </dev/null`) so any stray read fails fast instead of blocking.
  - Redirect stdout/stderr to the persistent log file so writes cannot fail with `EIO` on a dead pty.
  - Keep a dedicated FD open on the operator's TTY for the progress box/spinner (`PROGRESS_UI_FD`). Detailed command output (apt, wg-quick, systemctl) stays in the log ONLY.
  - Do **not** auto-tail the full log onto the TTY — that interleaves with the spinner and makes the UI unreadable. Tell the operator they can `tail -f` the log themselves if they want the raw stream.
- Never add an interactive prompt after the input phase. Any new question must move into the input-collection block.
- Before detaching, surface every known disconnect risk in a single consolidated warning:
  - SSH session bound to LAN/WAN that will be reconfigured.
  - Pi Connect (or similar daemon) active.
  - WireGuard `AllowedIPs` containing a default route (`0.0.0.0/0`, `::/0`, or `0.0.0.0/1` + `128.0.0.0/1`) - this redirects all outbound traffic into the tunnel and almost always breaks remote management unless the peer NATs the Pi's traffic.
- The progress UI may degrade if the TTY disappears mid-run (writes to the UI FD fail silently) — that is acceptable. Correctness of the unattended run is the priority.
